window.COURSE={"pages":[{"key":"home","file":"sql/index.html","name":"SQL home","short":"SQL","band":"","sections":[]},{"key":"s00","file":"sql/00-foundations.html","name":"SQL 00 · Foundations","short":"S00","band":"sqlfound","sections":[{"id":"s-what","n":"S1","t":"What a database is"},{"id":"s-schema","n":"S2","t":"Tables, keys and the dealership schema"},{"id":"s-select","n":"S3","t":"Your first query: SELECT … FROM"},{"id":"s-order","n":"S4","t":"How a query really runs: the logical order"},{"id":"s-keys","n":"S5","t":"Primary keys, foreign keys and NULL"},{"id":"s-review","n":"S6","t":"Foundations: interview questions and review"}]}],"qa":[{"k":"s-review:Q1","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"What is the difference between a primary key and a foreign key?","a":"A primary key uniquely identifies a row in its own table: unique and never NULL, one per table. A foreign key is a column in one table that holds the primary key of a row in another (or the same) table; it is how tables relate, and the database refuses values that point at no row."},{"k":"s-review:Q2","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"In what order are the clauses of a SELECT executed?","a":"FROM (and JOIN), WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT/OFFSET. That is why WHERE cannot use a SELECT alias or an aggregate, HAVING can use aggregates, and ORDER BY can use the alias."},{"k":"s-review:Q3","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"WHERE vs HAVING?","a":"WHERE filters individual rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY, so it can use COUNT, SUM and friends. Put a condition in WHERE whenever it does not need an aggregate: it removes rows earlier and is cheaper."},{"k":"s-review:Q4","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"Why does <code>WHERE city = NULL</code> return nothing?","a":"NULL is \"unknown\". Any comparison with unknown is unknown, not true, and WHERE keeps only rows whose condition is true. Use <code>city IS NULL</code> or <code>IS NOT NULL</code>."},{"k":"s-review:Q5","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"COUNT(*) vs COUNT(column) vs COUNT(DISTINCT column)?","a":"COUNT(*) counts rows. COUNT(col) counts rows where col is not NULL. COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts different non-NULL values. On <code>services</code>: 8, 6 and (for cost) 6."},{"k":"s-review:Q6","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"What does <code>SELECT *</code> cost you in application code?","a":"The result's shape depends on the table: a new column changes every caller, reads more bytes than needed, and can defeat a covering index. Name the columns."},{"k":"s-review:Q7","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"Is the row order of a query without ORDER BY defined?","a":"No. Engines return rows in whatever order the plan produced them (table order, index order, hash order), and it can change with data size or a new index. Only ORDER BY guarantees an order; for pagination with LIMIT/OFFSET, always sort by something unique."},{"k":"s-review:Q8","l":"S00","sec":"s-review","st":"Foundations: interview questions and review","q":"What is a schema?","a":"The design of the database: which tables exist, their columns and types, their keys and constraints, and how they reference each other. (In PostgreSQL \"schema\" is also a namespace inside a database, like <code>public</code>.)"}]};
